description:
## Downstream observation (2026-09-10)

A native Rust/SQLx API migrated serving acquisition to Startup at c6efc176efe7b5d67acc61a91d352f9158429be7. Applying the same composition to a finite database setup command produced ShutdownCause::EmptySupervisor even though its registered pool finalizer succeeded. The consumer correctly retained an OperationContext followed by explicitly awaited CleanupStack for the finite command, and uses Startup for serving. No dummy component or success override was retained.

This is a documentation/composition gap, not evidence that EmptySupervisor is a runtime defect. crates/batter/src/startup.rs shows resource acquisition/cleanup and assert!(report.is_success()) with a hidden caller-supplied Supervisor, without explaining that successful running supervision requires critical components or configured finite-work capacity. The example is no_run. Source at 188c389 retains the same example and empty-supervisor rule.

## Existing scope checked

batter-7r3.3 delivered owned service startup and handoff, and is closed. batter-v20 delivered bounded finite work admitted by an already Ready process; it is not a standalone pre-readiness acquisition/finalization owner. reserve_finalization divides budgets but does not independently drive abandoned cleanup. Supervisor::with_process_capacity supports supervision without critical components; finite work still requires explicit submission and shutdown. EmptySupervisor applies only when both critical components and finite-work capacity are absent. Enabling unused capacity solely to bypass that result is not the recommended finite-command composition. No dedicated finite-command composition guidance task was found in the current open/closed inventory.

## Reproduction

Executed on Linux, Rust 1.97.1, Tokio 1.53.1, exact Batter Git pin c6efc176efe7b5d67acc61a91d352f9158429be7. A minimal binary with tokio rt/macros features, no database or external service, runs the following. This is a fresh focused reproduction, separate from the consumer's prior live database test run.

```rust
use batter::{cleanup::CleanupBudget, lifecycle::{ShutdownBudget, Supervisor}, operation::OperationContext, startup::Startup};
use std::time::Duration;
#[tokio::main(flavor = "current_thread")]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let second = Duration::from_secs(1);
    let cleanup = CleanupBudget::new(second, second, second)?;
    let supervisor = Supervisor::new(ShutdownBudget::new(second, second, second, cleanup)?);
    let mut starting = Startup::new(supervisor, OperationContext::new(second)?, cleanup, |scope| Box::pin(async move {
        scope.stage("resource")?;
        let slot = scope.supervisor().reserve_cleanup("resource")?;
        let resource = String::from("acquired");
        slot.register(move || async move { drop(resource); Ok(()) });
        Ok::<_, batter::RegistrationError>(())
    })).start();
    let running = starting.wait().await?;
    let report = running.shutdown().await?;
    println!("cause={:?} success={} cleanup={:?}", report.cause, report.is_success(), report.cleanup);
    Ok(())
}

```

Observed: cause=EmptySupervisor success=false; cleanup contains resource=Succeeded and no skipped finalizers. Expected current service contract: exactly that result. Expected documentation: make the precondition and the appropriate finite-command composition clear before a consumer copies the pattern.

## Requested direction

Make the service-startup example executable with genuine registered service work and explicit ownership/readiness semantics. Add a minimal finite-command example using native acquisition, pre-reserved cleanup registration, a bounded operation, and separately awaited cleanup with both failures retained. Explain immediate command cancellation versus service drain. State that cancellation of the finite command's own cleanup waiter is not covered by Startup ownership.

If independently driven finalization for an abandoned standalone command is justified by a concrete second use, evaluate the narrowest composition with existing owners in a separately scoped follow-up. Do not weaken EmptySupervisor, introduce fake services, promise asynchronous Drop, or build a second runtime to repair this documentation gap. Keep runtime-death and remote database termination exclusions explicit.
